0:{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[[["$","$L2","4.12",{"topic":{"id":"4.12","title":"Courtroom Presentation and Expert Testimony","minutes":13,"summary":"An expert assists the court with specialist reasoning, stays within expertise, and makes methods and limitations understandable.","sections":[{"title":"The expert’s role and preparation","body":["BSA section 39 addresses opinions of persons specially skilled in relevant fields, while the old Evidence Act section 45 played the corresponding role. The facts supporting the opinion and the expert’s qualifications matter. Expert status does not make every assertion true, and the expert should not decide the accused’s guilt.","Prepare by reviewing the report, underlying exhibits, methods, limitations, and relevant professional guidance. Be able to explain why a method was chosen, how it was validated, what changed during acquisition, and what another examiner could verify. Distinguish your own observations from information supplied by investigators or third-party tools."]},{"title":"Explain, qualify, and acknowledge limits","body":["Use plain language and a small number of well-supported visuals. Define technical terms, identify the source of each important fact, and explain the difference between consistency and conclusive proof. If a question moves beyond your expertise or the examined material, say so. A careful “I cannot determine that from this evidence” can be the most accurate answer.","Do not exaggerate tool reliability or claim a zero error rate without a defensible basis. Explain known errors, alternative explanations, and material missing data. Where the law requires a certificate or particular witness, technical expertise alone does not replace that requirement. Coordinate with counsel on procedure without becoming an advocate for a party."]},{"title":"Professional independence and updates","body":["The duty is to assist the court, not to win the case. Maintain independence, disclose material limitations, and correct an opinion if new evidence changes it. Keep a clear record of supplements and the reason for revision. Do not allow cross-examination pressure to turn a qualified conclusion into an unsupported absolute.","Protect sensitive exhibits during presentation and follow the court’s directions for access, redaction, and return. After testimony, preserve the report and relevant notes under the applicable retention process. The learning goal is not theatrical confidence; it is a transparent account that remains reliable when questioned."]}],"practice":{"title":"Answer the difficult question","scenario":"Counsel asks: “Your tool says the file was deleted. Can you swear the accused deleted it?”","steps":["Separate the filesystem observation from human attribution.","Explain the tool’s role and the relevant limitations.","Identify evidence that would be needed for attribution.","Give a concise, neutral answer without speculation."],"debrief":"A strong expert answer explains what is known, how it is known, and what the evidence cannot establish."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bsa","iea"],"sources":["bsa","iea","cert"],"labId":"certificate","unit":4,"objectives":["Explain the expert’s role and preparation and apply it to an investigation.","Explain explain, qualify, and acknowledge limits and apply it to an investigation.","Explain professional independence and updates and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-4.12","prompt":"What should an expert do when asked beyond the examined evidence?","options":["Guess confidently.","State the limitation and remain within supported expertise.","Declare the accused guilty.","Hide missing data."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Expert credibility depends on transparent limits and independence, not certainty on every question.","unit":4}}}]],["$L3"],"$L4"]}],"isPartial":false,"staleTime":300,"varyParams":null,"buildId":"mMiCr23A_-Cpk0hZkfT7X"}
